.TH "notcurses\-info" "1" "v3.0.17" ""
.SH "نام (NAME)"
notcurses\-info \- Display information about the terminal environment
.SH "خلاصه دستور (SYNOPSIS)"
\f[B]notcurses\-info\f[R] [\f[B]\-v\f[R]]
.SH "توضیحات (DESCRIPTION)"
\f[B]notcurses\-info\f[R] prints all the information it knows about the
current terminal environment, including material loaded from
\f[B]terminfo(5)\f[R] (based on the \f[B]TERM\f[R] environment
variable), replies from the terminal in response to our queries, and
built\-in heuristics.
.PP
The Unicode half block, quadrant, sextant, octant, and Braille glyphs
are all included in the output.
If their appearance is irregular, it might behoove you to choose another
font.
.PP
The first five lines (the Notcurses initialization banner; see
\f[B]notcurses_init(3)\f[R]) provide:
.IP \[bu] 2
The Notcurses version and the derived terminal name, possibly including
the terminal version.
If Notcurses was able to unambiguously query the connected terminal, the
internal name for the terminal will be shown.
Otherwise, the terminal described by the \f[B]TERM\f[R] environment
variable will be displayed.
The terminal version is only acquired via query.
.IP \[bu] 2
The current cell geometry, cell\-pixel geometry, and the derived window
pixel geometry, the size of the \f[B]crender\f[R] structure, the number
of colors in the palette, and whether RGB TrueColor is supported.
.IP \[bu] 2
The compiler name and version used to build Notcurses, the size of the
\f[B]nccell\f[R] structure, and the endianness with which Notcurses was
compiled.
This buildtime endianness must match the runtime endianness.
.IP \[bu] 2
The version of libterminfo against which Notcurses was compiled.
.IP \[bu] 2
The version and name of the multimedia backend.
.PP
The next five lines describe properties of the terminal environment:
.IP \[bu] 2
The first line indicates that a given capability is present with a plus
sign (\[aq]+\[aq]), or not present/detected with a minus sign
(\[aq]\-\[aq]):
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
af: Foreground color can be set
.IP \[bu] 2
ab: Background color can be set
.IP \[bu] 2
sum: Synchronized Update Mode is supported
.IP \[bu] 2
vpa: Cursor can be moved to an absolute vertical coordinate
.IP \[bu] 2
hpa: Cursor can be moved to an absolute horizontal coordinate
.IP \[bu] 2
sgr0: Styling can be reset via a single escape
.IP \[bu] 2
op: Colors can be reset via a single escape
.IP \[bu] 2
fgop: Foreground can be reset via a single escape
.IP \[bu] 2
bgop: Background can be reset via a single escape
.IP \[bu] 2
bce: The back\-color\-erase property is in play
.IP \[bu] 2
rect: Rectangular editing is available
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
The second line is more of the same:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
bold: Boldface is available
.IP \[bu] 2
ital: Italics are available
.IP \[bu] 2
struck: Strikethrough is available
.IP \[bu] 2
ucurl: Curled underlines are available
.IP \[bu] 2
uline: Straight underlines are available
.IP \[bu] 2
u7: Cursor position reporting
.IP \[bu] 2
ccc: Palette can be reprogrammed
.IP \[bu] 2
rgb: Colors can be specified as RGB wit eight bits/channel
.IP \[bu] 2
el: Clearing can be performed through the end of the line
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
The third line also covers UTF8 and decoding capabilities:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
utf8: This is a UTF8 environment
.IP \[bu] 2
2x1: Upper\- and lower\-half blocks are available
.IP \[bu] 2
2x2: Quadrant blocks are available
.IP \[bu] 2
3x2: Sextant blocks are available
.IP \[bu] 2
4x2: Octant blocks are available
.IP \[bu] 2
4x2: Braille characters are available
.IP \[bu] 2
img: Images can be decoded
.IP \[bu] 2
vid: Video can be decoded
.IP \[bu] 2
indn: Multiple\-line scrolling is available
.IP \[bu] 2
gpm: Connection is established to the GPM server
.IP \[bu] 2
kbd: The Kitty keyboard protocol is in use
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
The fourth line indicates the default background color, and whether that
color is treated as transparent by the terminal (only \f[B]kitty\f[R] is
known to do this), and the default foreground color.
\f[B]pmouse\f[R] indicates whether pixel\-precise mouse events are
supported.
.IP \[bu] 2
The fifth line describes the available bitmap graphics.
If Sixels are available, the maximum number of color registers and
maximum Sixel geometry are reported.
If Linux framebuffer graphics are available, that is reported.
If the Kitty graphics protocol is detected, that will be reported with
\[dq]rgba graphics are available\[dq]; if Kitty\[aq]s animation support
is also present, that will be reported with \[dq]rgba pixel animation
support\[dq].
.PP
To the right of this material is the Notcurses homepage\[aq]s URI, and
the Notcurses logo (the latter only if bitmap graphics are available).
.PP
The final eleven lines, only printed when in a UTF8 locale, show various
Unicode glyphs.
The first four lines include the quadrant, sextant, octant, and
box\-drawing characters.
The next four lines include the entire Braille set.
The following two lines include many of the Symbols for Legacy Computing
introduced in Unicode 13.
The final line includes many emoji.
.SH "گزینه‌ها (OPTIONS)"
\f[B]\-v\f[R]: Be verbose.
.SH "نکات (NOTES)"
The behavior of \f[B]notcurses\-info\f[R] (and indeed all of Notcurses)
depends on the \f[B]TERM\f[R] and \f[B]LANG\f[R] environment variables,
the installed POSIX locales, and the installed \f[B]terminfo(5)\f[R]
databases.
.SH "همچنین ببینید (SEE ALSO)"
\f[B]tack(1)\f[R], \f[B]notcurses(3)\f[R], \f[B]terminfo(5)\f[R]
